{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QuantumUniverses.PoissonObject","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QuantumUniverses.PoissonObject","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverse.GaugeTheory","lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverse.CategoricalFoundations","lean:module:QOU.Basic","lean:module:QOU.Glossary","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Monoidal.Category","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Monoidal.Braided.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Preadditive.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Functor.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.NatTrans"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject.jacobi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject.jacobi","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L86-L86","doc":"**Jacobi identity**: the Jacobiator vanishes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject.jacobiator","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject.jacobiator","kind":"def","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L83-L83","doc":"The Jacobiator: categorical morphism `(A ⊗ A) ⊗ A → A`\nencoding the cyclic sum `{f,{g,h}} + {g,{h,f}} + {h,{f,g}}`.\nProvided abstractly since the cyclic sum involves iterated\nbrackets and associator shuffles. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject.leibniz","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject.leibniz","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L78-L78","doc":"**Leibniz rule**: `{f, g·h} = {f,g}·h + g·{f,h}`.\nStated as an equality of morphisms `A ⊗ (A ⊗ A) → A`:\nthe bracket applied after multiplication equals the abstract\nLeibniz RHS. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject.leibniz_rhs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject.leibniz_rhs","kind":"def","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L72-L72","doc":"The Leibniz-rule RHS: categorical morphism `A ⊗ (A ⊗ A) → A`\nencoding `{f,g}·h + g·{f,h}`.  Provided abstractly following\nthe pattern of `CategoricalConnection.leibniz_rhs`, since the\nfull composite involves associators and whiskerings that depend\non the specific monoidal structure.  In a strict monoidal\ncategory this equals\n`(bracket ⊗ id) ≫ μ + (id ⊗ bracket) ≫ μ` (suitably shuffled). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject.bracket","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject.bracket","kind":"def","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L64-L64","doc":"The bracket morphism `{–, –} : A ⊗ A → A` obtained by\ncontracting `π` against differentials.  This is the composite\n`A ⊗ A →[d ⊗ d] Ω¹ ⊗ Ω¹ →[∧] Ω² →[⟨π, –⟩] A`.\nAnti-symmetric by construction since `π ∈ Λ²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject.bivector","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject.bivector","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L59-L59","doc":"The Poisson bivector `π : 𝟙 → Π` (a global section). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject.bivectorObj","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject.bivectorObj","kind":"def","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L57-L57","doc":"The bivector object `Π = Λ²(Ω¹(A)∨)` in `C`.\nUnder Frobenius self-duality this is isomorphic to `Ω²(A)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject.mk","kind":"ctor","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L49-L49","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.PoissonObject","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.PoissonObject","kind":"structure","line":33,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.lean#L33-L86","doc":"**Internal Poisson object** in a braided preadditive monoidal category.\n\nGiven Frobenius–Hopf data `(A, μ, η, Δ, ε, S, λ)` and a\ndifferential calculus `(Ω¹, ∇)`, a Poisson object consists of a\nbivector object `Π` (representing `Λ²(Ω¹(A)∨)`) and a global\nsection `π : 𝟙 → Π` satisfying the Jacobi identity and Leibniz\nrule.  Anti-symmetry of the bracket is automatic since `π` factors\nthrough `Λ²`.\n\nThe bracket is defined by the pullback composite:\n  `{f, g} := ⟨π, df ∧ dg⟩`\nwhere the contraction uses the Frobenius isomorphism `A ≅ A∨`\nto identify `Ω¹(A)∨ ≅ Ω¹(A)`.\n\nLaTeX: `\\label{def:poisson-object}`\nRef: [kock2004] ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:def:poisson-object","label":"def:poisson-object","md":"quantum-observable-universe/quantum-universes/poisson-object.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}